These pages are charter's `docs/*.md` as the WHEEL ships them — `charter/_docs` — taken from

    https://github.com/diazoxide/charter  @ 50d31dc66835592ccea625bab8f5a0da313f2444

which is the same commit `tests/differential/run.py` pins its Python oracle to. That is not a
coincidence and it is not documentation: it is the only thing tying the two copies together.
`docs show <topic>` is compared byte for byte against that oracle, one scenario per topic, and
`check_docs_corpus` compares the two corpora as digests before any scenario runs — so a page
that drifts here, or is dropped, or is added, turns the differential red rather than printing
a page charter would not have printed.

TWO PAGES ARE DELIBERATELY NOT THE ORACLE'S, AND THAT IS DECLARED

`control-plane.md` and `install.md` describe what `charter init` does at the top of an existing
git repository, and ADR 0035 reversed that for charter-app: the plane goes in a directory of
its own, the repo becomes its first clone, and `--plane-is-this-repo` is the opt-in. The Python
charter is frozen (spec decision 17), so the pages cannot be corrected upstream — and a frozen
oracle documenting behaviour it does not have would be worse than the drift. Each page says in
its own text which implementation it is describing, which is the condition charter-app#119 puts
on holding a different copy at all.

`DOCS_DIVERGE` in `tests/differential/run.py` is where that is declared, page by page and
passage by passage. It is NOT a skip: the run rewrites the oracle's page with the declared
passages and compares the result byte for byte, so everything the declaration does not name is
held to exactly the bar it always was — and the declaration itself fails loudly if charter stops
saying its half, if charter-app stops saying its half, or if the two copies ever agree again.

`docsrc.rs` carries the cheap half of the same claim, so a page that offers
`charter init --clone-this-repo` fails `cargo test` rather than waiting for the differential.

THE WHEEL, NOT THE REPOSITORY

`charter/docsrc.py` resolves `charter/_docs` first and falls back to the repository's own
`docs/` for a contributor running out of a checkout. The wheel is what everyone else gets, so
the wheel is what is vendored — and the two are not the same statement, because
`pyproject.toml` force-includes the pages ONE BY ONE rather than as a directory, to keep
`adr/`, `audits/` and `superpowers/` out of site-packages. A page that exists in the
repository and has no line there is a page `charter docs show` does not have, on every machine
that installed charter rather than cloning it. At this commit the two lists agree, page for
page; copying `docs/` would have been right today and silently wrong the first time a page
landed ahead of its force-include line.

WHY THERE ARE TWO COPIES AT ALL

`charter/docsrc.py` says it plainly: a control plane has no reason to vendor a copy and every
reason not to, because the page a user reads should come from the same install as the
behaviour — so `charter docs show secrets` cannot describe a vault the running CLI does not
have. The Python charter satisfies that with a wheel. A Rust charter shipping inside a signed
app has no checkout to fall back on, so the pages are compiled in: `build.rs` reads this
directory and `include_str!`s every file.

That leaves one copy per implementation for as long as both exist. It ends the way `news/`
does: when charter's documentation is authored here rather than in charter's repository, this
becomes the only copy.

NO FILE IN HERE IS NOT A PAGE

`build.rs` compiles in every `*.md` and `docsrc::topics` offers every one of them as a topic,
so a note about this directory has to be a file the glob does not match. This one is.
